+<section class="section section--alt" id="maturity" aria-labelledby="maturity-heading">
+  <div class="container">
+
+    <div class="section-eyebrow">
+      <span class="tag">// where are you today?</span>
+    </div>
+    <h2 id="maturity-heading" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
+      The AIOps <span class="text-green">Maturity Model</span>
+    </h2>
+    <p style="max-width:640px; margin-bottom:2rem;">
+      AIOps isn't a product you install. It's a set of capabilities you add one level at a time.
+      Skipping levels is the most common reason AIOps projects fail: an AI can't reason about
+      telemetry you don't collect or alerts nobody trusts.
+    </p>
+
+    <div class="maturity-ladder">
+      <div class="mat-level">
+        <div><div class="mat-num">Level 0</div><div class="mat-name">Reactive</div></div>
+        <div class="mat-col"><h4>What it looks like</h4><p>Users report outages before the team notices. Debugging means SSH-ing into servers and grepping logs.</p></div>
+        <div class="mat-col"><h4>Next step</h4><p>Collect metrics and centralise logs. Add uptime checks for every user-facing endpoint.</p></div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="mat-level">
+        <div><div class="mat-num">Level 1</div><div class="mat-name">Monitored</div></div>
+        <div class="mat-col"><h4>What it looks like</h4><p>Dashboards and static-threshold alerts (CPU &gt; 80%). Lots of noisy pages; some real issues still slip through.</p></div>
+        <div class="mat-col"><h4>Next step</h4><p>Define SLOs and alert on symptoms users feel (error rate, latency), not every resource metric.</p></div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="mat-level">
+        <div><div class="mat-num">Level 2</div><div class="mat-name">Correlated</div></div>
+        <div class="mat-col"><h4>What it looks like</h4><p>SLO burn-rate alerts, alerts grouped and de-duplicated, every alert linked to a runbook and enriched with recent deploys.</p></div>
+        <div class="mat-col"><h4>Next step</h4><p>Add dynamic baselines for metrics with daily or weekly patterns, and connect metrics, logs and traces.</p></div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="mat-level">
+        <div><div class="mat-num">Level 3</div><div class="mat-name">AI-assisted</div></div>
+        <div class="mat-col"><h4>What it looks like</h4><p>Anomaly detection catches what thresholds miss. An LLM agent gathers context when an alert fires and posts a triage summary. Humans decide and act.</p></div>
+        <div class="mat-col"><h4>Next step</h4><p>Measure how often the suggested cause is right. Automate only fixes that are well understood and reversible.</p></div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="mat-level">
+        <div><div class="mat-num">Level 4</div><div class="mat-name">Guarded autonomy</div></div>
+        <div class="mat-col"><h4>What it looks like</h4><p>Known, low-risk fixes (restart, rollback, scale out) run automatically within policy limits, with an audit trail, a kill switch and post-fix verification.</p></div>
