+<section class="section" id="guardrails" aria-labelledby="guardrails-heading">
+  <div class="container">
+
+    <div class="section-eyebrow">
+      <span class="tag">// safety first</span>
+    </div>
+    <h2 id="guardrails-heading" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
+      Guardrails Before AI <span class="text-green">Touches Production</span>
+    </h2>
+    <p style="max-width:640px; margin-bottom:2rem;">
+      An AI agent that can change production is a new kind of operator. Hold it to the same standards
+      as a human with production access, and stricter ones where it's cheap to do so.
+    </p>
+
+    <ul class="guard-list">
+      <li><strong>Read-only by default.</strong> Investigation needs no write access. Grant writes one narrow action at a time.</li>
+      <li><strong>Its own identity.</strong> A dedicated service account and cloud role, never a human's credentials or cluster-admin.</li>
+      <li><strong>Allowlisted actions.</strong> "Restart deployment X in namespace Y", not a shell. Validate every argument before running it.</li>
+      <li><strong>Human approval for changes.</strong> Show the evidence and the exact command, and wait for a person to approve.</li>
+      <li><strong>Blast-radius limits.</strong> Cap how many actions it can take per hour, and never act on more than one service at a time.</li>
+      <li><strong>Audit everything.</strong> Log every tool call, input, output and decision, so you can review them in postmortems.</li>
+      <li><strong>Kill switch.</strong> One flag that turns all automated actions off instantly.</li>
+      <li><strong>Treat telemetry as untrusted.</strong> Log lines and alert text can contain prompt-injection attempts. Never let them grant the agent new permissions.</li>
+      <li><strong>Verify after acting.</strong> Check that the SLIs recovered, and roll back the agent's own change if they didn't.</li>
+    </ul>
+
+  </div>
+</section>
