+    <h1>Anomaly Detection with PromQL: Beyond Static Thresholds</h1>
+    <p class="post-lede">Dynamic baselines with z-scores, weekly seasonality, forecasting with predict_linear, and anomaly alert rules that don't cry wolf, using only Prometheus.</p>
+    <div class="post-meta">
+      <span>📉 AI &amp; AIOps</span>
+      <span>📅 <time datetime="2026-11-22">22 Nov 2026</time></span>
+      <span>⏱ 5 min read</span>
+      <span>📶 Intermediate</span>
+    </div>
+    <div class="post-tags"><span class="post-tag">AIOps</span><span class="post-tag">Prometheus</span><span class="post-tag">Anomaly Detection</span></div>
+  </div>
+</header>
+
+<div class="container">
+  <div class="post-layout">
+    <article class="post-content">
+<p>"Alert when CPU is above 80%" is how most monitoring starts, and it's where most alert fatigue comes from. A fixed threshold is too sensitive at peak hours and blind at quiet ones. Anomaly detection asks a better question: <em>is this metric behaving differently from how it usually behaves?</em> You don't need an ML platform to answer it. Prometheus can do it with a few recording rules.</p>
+
+<div class="article-callout">
+  <p><strong>What you'll learn:</strong> dynamic baselines with z-scores, handling daily and weekly patterns, forecasting problems before they happen, and turning it all into alert rules that don't page you for nothing.</p>
+</div>
+
+<h2 id="why-static-thresholds-fail">Why static thresholds fail</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Traffic has rhythm.</strong> 500 requests per second may be normal at noon and alarming at 4am, yet a single threshold treats both the same.</li>
+  <li><strong>Services differ.</strong> One threshold for 40 services means it's wrong for most of them.</li>
+  <li><strong>Gradual failures hide.</strong> A memory leak that grows 2% an hour never crosses a threshold until it's too late.</li>
+  <li><strong>Drops matter too.</strong> A sudden fall in traffic (a broken load balancer, a bad DNS change) is often worse than a spike, and "above X" alerts never catch it.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2 id="step-1-record-the-base-signal">Step 1: Record the base signal</h2>
+<p>Anomaly queries compare "now" with long windows of history. Computing that from raw counters on every evaluation is expensive, so first record the signal once a minute with a <strong>recording rule</strong>:</p>
+<pre><code>groups:
+  - name: anomaly-detection
+    interval: 1m
+    rules:
+      - record: job:http_requests:rate5m
+        expr: sum by (job) (rate(http_requests_total[5m]))</code></pre>
+<p>Recorded series are cheap to query over long ranges, which is exactly what the next steps need.</p>
+
+<h2 id="step-2-dynamic-baselines-with-z-scores">Step 2: Dynamic baselines with z-scores</h2>
+<p>A <strong>z-score</strong> measures how far the current value is from the recent average, in units of standard deviation. Roughly: |z| &lt; 2 is normal, 2–3 is unusual, and above 3 is rare enough to look at.</p>
+<pre><code>      - record: job:http_requests:rate5m:avg_1d
+        expr: avg_over_time(job:http_requests:rate5m[1d])
+
+      - record: job:http_requests:rate5m:stddev_1d
+        expr: stddev_over_time(job:http_requests:rate5m[1d])
+
+      - record: job:http_requests:rate5m:zscore
+        expr: |
+          (job:http_requests:rate5m - job:http_requests:rate5m:avg_1d)
+            / (job:http_requests:rate5m:stddev_1d > 0)</code></pre>
+<p>The <code>&gt; 0</code> filter on the standard deviation avoids dividing by zero for perfectly flat series (a new service, or one with no traffic yet).</p>
+<p>Plot <code>job:http_requests:rate5m:zscore</code> in Grafana with horizontal lines at +3 and −3. You'll see each service's "normal" band without having to pick a threshold for each one.</p>
+
+<h2 id="step-3-handle-daily-and-weekly-patterns">Step 3: Handle daily and weekly patterns</h2>
+<p>A 24-hour average blends day and night, so the Monday-morning ramp-up looks like an anomaly every week. Compare with the <strong>same time in previous weeks</strong> instead:</p>
+<pre><code># current traffic as a ratio of the same time last week
+job:http_requests:rate5m
+  / job:http_requests:rate5m offset 1w
+
+# more robust: compare with the average of the last three weeks
+job:http_requests:rate5m
+/ (
+    (
+        job:http_requests:rate5m offset 1w
+      + job:http_requests:rate5m offset 2w
+      + job:http_requests:rate5m offset 3w
+    ) / 3
+  )</code></pre>
+<p>A ratio of 1.0 means "the same as usual for this hour and weekday". Below 0.5 or above 2 is worth a look. Averaging several weeks keeps one unusual week (a holiday or an earlier incident) from skewing the baseline.</p>
+
+<div class="article-callout warn">
+  <p><strong>Retention matters:</strong> <code>offset 3w</code> needs at least three weeks of data. Check your Prometheus <code>--storage.tsdb.retention.time</code>, or use long-term storage (Thanos, Mimir) for these queries.</p>
+</div>
+
+<h2 id="step-4-forecast-problems-before-they-happen">Step 4: Forecast problems before they happen</h2>
+<p><code>predict_linear()</code> fits a straight line through recent data and extrapolates it. It's ideal for slow, steady failures:</p>
+<pre><code># disk will be full within 4 hours, based on the last 6 hours
+predict_linear(node_filesystem_avail_bytes{fstype!="tmpfs"}[6h], 4 * 3600) &lt; 0
+
+# container memory will hit its limit within 1 hour (memory leak)
+predict_linear(container_memory_working_set_bytes{container!=""}[1h], 3600)
+  &gt; on (namespace, pod, container)
+    kube_pod_container_resource_limits{resource="memory"}</code></pre>
+<p>A forecast alert gives you hours of warning instead of minutes, and turns a 3am incident into a ticket for the morning.</p>
+<p>Prometheus also has <code>double_exponential_smoothing()</code> (called <code>holt_winters()</code> before Prometheus 3.0) for smoothing noisy series. In Prometheus 3.x it's experimental and must be enabled with the <code>promql-experimental-functions</code> feature flag.</p>
+
+<h2 id="step-5-turn-it-into-alerts-that-don-t-cry-wolf">Step 5: Turn it into alerts that don't cry wolf</h2>
+<p>Raw anomaly scores are noisy. Three guards make them usable:</p>
+<ol>
+  <li><strong>A minimum volume:</strong> going from 0.1 to 0.5 requests per second is a 5× jump, but it doesn't matter. Require real traffic before alerting.</li>
+  <li><strong>A duration (<code>for:</code>):</strong> only alert if the anomaly lasts, not on one odd scrape.</li>
+  <li><strong>Warning severity:</strong> anomalies are hints, so send them to a channel or ticket and keep paging for SLO burn-rate alerts.</li>
+</ol>
+<pre><code>      - alert: TrafficAnomaly
+        expr: |
+          abs(job:http_requests:rate5m:zscore) &gt; 3
+          and job:http_requests:rate5m &gt; 1
+        for: 10m
+        labels:
+          severity: warning
+        annotations:
+          summary: "Unusual traffic for {{ $labels.job }}"
+          description: "Request rate is {{ $value | printf \"%.1f\" }} standard deviations from its 24h average."
+
+      - alert: DiskWillFillIn4Hours
+        expr: |
+          predict_linear(node_filesystem_avail_bytes{fstype!="tmpfs"}[6h], 4 * 3600) &lt; 0
+          and node_filesystem_avail_bytes{fstype!="tmpfs"}
+            / node_filesystem_size_bytes{fstype!="tmpfs"} &lt; 0.2
+        for: 15m
+        labels:
+          severity: warning
+        annotations:
+          summary: "Disk on {{ $labels.instance }} ({{ $labels.mountpoint }}) is predicted to fill within 4h"</code></pre>
+<p>The second condition on the disk alert (less than 20% free) stops brief write bursts on a mostly empty disk from triggering it.</p>
+
+<h2 id="step-6-test-your-rules">Step 6: Test your rules</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Check syntax</strong> in CI with <code>promtool check rules anomaly-rules.yml</code>.</li>
+  <li><strong>Unit-test</strong> the logic with <code>promtool test rules</code>: you describe input series and the alerts you expect, with no live cluster needed.</li>
+  <li><strong>Backtest</strong> by pasting each expression into Grafana over the last 30 days. Count how often it would have fired, and how many of those were real problems.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2 id="where-this-fits-in-aiops">Where this fits in AIOps</h2>
+<p>Anomaly detection is level 2–3 of the <a href="aiops.html#maturity">AIOps maturity model</a>: it catches problems static thresholds miss. Pair it with an <a href="blog-llm-triage-agent.html">LLM triage agent</a>, which gathers context when an anomaly fires, and with <a href="blog-sre-fundamentals.html">SLO burn-rate alerts</a>, which decide when to wake someone up.</p>
+<ul>
+  <li>Practise in <a href="projects.html#observability">Project 06: Observability Stack</a>.</li>
+  <li>Keep the <a href="cheatsheets.html#prometheus">PromQL cheatsheet</a> handy.</li>
+</ul>
