+    <section class="qa-group" id="aiops">
+      <h2><span>🤖</span> AIOps &amp; AI in Ops <span class="qa-count">(6)</span></h2>
+      <details class="qa-item" id="what-is-aiops-and-how-is-it-different-from-traditional-monit" data-cat="aiops" data-lvl="B">
+        <summary><span class="qa-lvl B">Beginner</span><span>What is AIOps, and how is it different from traditional monitoring?</span></summary>
+        <div class="qa-answer"><p>Traditional monitoring alerts when a metric crosses a threshold a human chose. <strong>AIOps</strong> applies statistics, ML and LLMs to operations data to <strong>detect</strong> anomalies thresholds miss, <strong>correlate</strong> many alerts into one incident, <strong>diagnose</strong> a likely root cause and, carefully, <strong>remediate</strong>. It builds on good observability rather than replacing it: an AI can't reason about telemetry you don't collect.</p></div>
+      </details>
+      <details class="qa-item" id="how-would-you-detect-anomalies-in-a-metric-with-strong-daily" data-cat="aiops" data-lvl="I">
+        <summary><span class="qa-lvl I">Intermediate</span><span>How would you detect anomalies in a metric with strong daily and weekly patterns?</span></summary>
+        <div class="qa-answer"><p>A single static threshold or a plain 24-hour average will flag every daily peak. Options, from simplest:</p><ul><li><strong>Seasonal comparison:</strong> compare with the same time last week, e.g. <code>rate(x[5m]) / rate(x[5m] offset 1w)</code>, ideally averaged over several weeks.</li><li><strong>Z-score per time window</strong> against a baseline built from matching hours.</li><li><strong>Seasonal models</strong> (Holt-Winters, Prophet) when you need forecasts.</li></ul><p>Then add guards: minimum traffic volume, a <code>for:</code> duration, and warning severity rather than paging.</p></div>
+      </details>
+      <details class="qa-item" id="what-is-alert-correlation-and-how-do-you-reduce-alert-noise" data-cat="aiops" data-lvl="I">
+        <summary><span class="qa-lvl I">Intermediate</span><span>What is alert correlation, and how do you reduce alert noise?</span></summary>
+        <div class="qa-answer"><p>One root cause (a failing database or node) triggers many symptom alerts. Correlation groups them into one incident:</p><ul><li>Alertmanager <code>group_by</code> on labels like service and namespace, plus <code>group_wait</code></li><li><code>inhibit_rules</code> to suppress symptoms while the cause is firing (e.g. node down silences pod alerts on that node)</li><li>Topology or dependency maps to link alerts across services</li><li>Alerting on SLO burn rate instead of every resource metric</li></ul></div>
+      </details>
+      <details class="qa-item" id="how-would-you-design-an-llm-agent-that-helps-with-incident-r" data-cat="aiops" data-lvl="A">
+        <summary><span class="qa-lvl A">Advanced</span><span>How would you design an LLM agent that helps with incident response, and keep it safe?</span></summary>
+        <div class="qa-answer"><ol><li><strong>Start read-only:</strong> tools for PromQL queries, Kubernetes events, logs and recent deploys, under its own service account with no write verbs and no Secrets.</li><li><strong>Enforce limits in code, not in the prompt:</strong> namespace allowlists, argument validation, output size limits and an iteration cap.</li><li><strong>Structured output:</strong> summary, evidence, likely cause, suggested commands and confidence, so humans can check its reasoning.</li><li><strong>Measure accuracy</strong> against real incidents before adding any action.</li><li><strong>Add actions slowly:</strong> one reversible action at a time, behind human approval, with audit logs, rate limits, a kill switch and post-action verification.</li></ol><p>Treat logs and alert text as untrusted input, because they can contain prompt-injection attempts.</p></div>
+      </details>
+      <details class="qa-item" id="what-are-the-risks-of-auto-remediation-and-when-is-it-approp" data-cat="aiops" data-lvl="A">
+        <summary><span class="qa-lvl A">Advanced</span><span>What are the risks of auto-remediation, and when is it appropriate?</span></summary>
+        <div class="qa-answer"><p>Risks: acting on a wrong diagnosis, hiding a real bug (restarting a leaking pod forever), cascading changes across services, and loops where the fix triggers the alert again. Auto-remediation fits when the fault is <strong>well understood</strong>, the action is <strong>reversible and low-risk</strong> (restart, rollback, scale out within limits), its success can be <strong>verified automatically</strong>, and every action is logged and reviewed. Anything novel or risky stays with a human.</p></div>
+      </details>
+      <details class="qa-item" id="how-would-you-know-whether-an-aiops-tool-is-actually-helping" data-cat="aiops" data-lvl="I">
+        <summary><span class="qa-lvl I">Intermediate</span><span>How would you know whether an AIOps tool is actually helping?</span></summary>
+        <div class="qa-answer"><p>Measure it like any other change:</p><ul><li><strong>MTTD and MTTR</strong> before and after</li><li><strong>Alert volume and noise</strong>: pages per week, and the share that needed action</li><li><strong>Diagnosis accuracy</strong>: how often the suggested root cause matched the postmortem</li><li><strong>False positive and false negative rates</strong> for anomaly detection</li><li><strong>On-call feedback</strong>: did responders trust it and use it?</li></ul></div>
+      </details>
+    </section>
+
